<template>
  <div>
    <ProjectPageHeader project-id="insurance" context="HSBC" data-scroll-reveal>
      <h1>Insurance Product Configuration Engine</h1>
      <p class="project-subtitle">Mapping insurance requirements to the app’s screens and fields</p>
    </ProjectPageHeader>

    <article class="project-narrative" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">At <strong>HSBC</strong>, I developed an engine that automatically determines the screens and fields a banking app needs for an insurance product. Business requirements, design libraries, and engineering configuration each described a different part of that decision. I connected them through explicit rules, giving the team a tool that could generate the configuration and regenerate it as requirements changed.</p>
      <p>Across dozens of products in multiple markets, the required interface depended on both the product and the customer’s answers. Travel insurance might require trip dates, motor insurance might require vehicle details, and a follow-up question might apply only in a particular scenario. The tool needed to represent those conditions in a form the team could inspect and update.</p>

      <!-- 1 · THE PROBLEM -->
      <h2 class="project-chapter-first" id="first-the-problem" data-source-anchor="first-the-problem">One product, several descriptions</h2>
      <p>A change to a business requirement could affect the interface, even though the requirement and the available design components lived in separate documents. Bringing the documents together would still leave the relationship between them implicit. I needed to represent that relationship directly, so the engine could work out which components followed from a product’s specification.</p>
      <p>A direct list of components for each product would miss the effect of an earlier answer on a later field. I represented those conditions as business scenarios, creating an intermediate step between the product specification and the components it required. The engine could evaluate which scenarios applied before selecting the interface.</p>

      <!-- 2 · THE IDEA -->
      <h2 id="the-idea" data-source-anchor="the-idea">Making the mapping inspectable</h2>
      <p>The engine follows the mapping <strong>product specification → business scenarios → UI-component requirements</strong>. Decision tables express which specification values make a scenario apply and which components that scenario requires. The three libraries below separate the product information, available components, and rules that connect them.</p>
      <div class="lib-cards" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="120">
        <div class="lib-card">
          <span class="lib-tag">Product specification</span>
          <p>The business fields, values, and conditional inputs that describe what the product requires.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lib-card">
          <span class="lib-tag">UI components</span>
          <p>The reusable screens, fields, and interface components available in the app.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lib-card">
          <span class="lib-tag">Decision tables</span>
          <p>The conditions that map specification values to the UI-component requirements for each business scenario.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>This separation lets a requirement, component, or rule be revised in its own library. Running the engine again evaluates the updated specification against the decision tables and produces the corresponding configuration.</p>

      <!-- 3 · THE PLAYGROUND -->
      <h2 id="try-it" data-source-anchor="try-it">Try the rules on a trip</h2>
      <p class="play-intro">
        This interactive example uses trip planning to show the mapping without disclosing insurance data. The trip
        inputs stand in for a product specification, the rule columns represent business scenarios, and packing items
        replace UI components. Change an input and follow its effect through the highlighted rules to the packing list.
      </p>

      <InsuranceRuleEngine />

      <!-- 4 · AUTOMATED CONFIGURATION -->
      <h2 id="from-the-packing-demo-to-the-production-tool" data-source-anchor="from-the-packing-demo-to-the-production-tool">Automating product configuration</h2>
      <p>In the production Excel/VBA tool, users entered product specifications through a dynamic form, and the engine automatically applied the decision tables to generate the required UI-component configuration. The VBA engine was roughly 32 KB, with a small notation describing when follow-up fields should appear. To handle both broad defaults and specific exceptions, the decision tables supported wildcard matching and right-side overrides, allowing specific cases to take precedence over general rules.</p>
      <p>Automating the mapping also made the rules easier to revisit. When a requirement changed, the team could update its specification or decision table and regenerate the configuration. The selected components could be traced back through the scenarios that required them. I developed four versions of the tool and included an embedded tutorial so the team could continue maintaining those rules after my internship.</p>
